    attributes: {
      'gen_ai.operation.name': 'execute_tool',
      'gen_ai.tool.name': 'bash',
      'gen_ai.tool.call.id': 'toolu_01DNCgdYSfSFTrSACyYZCXQv',
      'gen_ai.tool.type': 'function',
      'gen_ai.provider.name': 'github',
      'gen_ai.tool.call.arguments': '{"command":"mkdir -p .fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory && cat .fractal-factory/domain-brief.md 2>/dev/null | head -50 || echo \\"NOT FOUND\\"","description":"Check domain brief and create agent dir","initial_wait":5}',
      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '# Fantasy Fiction Writer — Domain Brief\n' +
        '\n' +
        '## Purpose\n' +
        '\n' +
        'Build an autonomous multi-agent system that writes **romantic fantasy fiction** — from initial concept through fully-drafted, reviewed, and polished story chapters. The system takes raw inputs (story ideas, mood boards, reference stories, style samples) and produces complete fiction through a structured creative pipeline that mirrors how professional authors and editorial teams work.\n' +
        '\n' +
        '## Genre Focus: Romantic Fantasy\n' +
        '\n' +
        'Romantic fantasy is a subgenre of fantasy where the central plot is a fantasy narrative (quests, magic systems, political intrigue, world-altering conflict) but a romantic relationship between protagonists is a major throughline that enriches and complicates the main plot. Unlike "fantasy romance" (which is romance-first), romantic fantasy keeps the fantasy arc primary while the romance serves as emotional spine.\n' +
        '\n' +
        '### Key Genre Characteristics\n' +
        '- **Fantasy-first plot**: Epic quests, magical conflicts, political intrigue, world-threatening stakes\n' +
        "- **Romance as emotional spine**: The central relationship drives character growth and raises personal stakes, but doesn't supersede the fantasy plot\n" +
        '- **Slow-burn preferred**: Tension builds across chapters; emotional and physical intimacy escalate gradually\n' +
        '- **Popular tropes**: Enemies-to-lovers, forbidden love, fated mates, forced proximity, secret identity, there-was-only-one-bed, morally grey love interests, found family\n' +
        '- **Worldbuilding depth**: Rich magic systems, detailed cultures, complex political structures, immersive settings\n' +
        '- **Character complexity**: Protagonists with deep backstories, psychological wounds, competing loyalties, and genuine growth arcs\n' +
        '- **Dual POV common**: Alternating perspectives between the romantic leads\n' +
        '- **HEA/HFN ending**: "Happily ever after" or "happy for now" — the genre promises emotional satisfaction\n' +
        '\n' +
        '### Representative Works (Style/Tone References)\n' +
        '- **Sarah J. Maas** — *A Court of Thorns and Roses* series (lush prose, slow-burn, high stakes, spice)\n' +
        '- **Leigh Bardugo** — *Six of Crows* (ensemble cast, heist plot with romance woven through)\n' +
        '- **Samantha Shannon** — *The Priory of the Orange Tree* (epic scope, F/F romance, complex worldbuilding)\n' +
        '- **Nalini Singh** — *Guild Hunter* series (paranormal romance-fantasy, compelling world, tension)\n' +
        '- **Jay Kristoff** — *Nevernight* (dark, gory, assassin school, smutty undertones)\n' +
        '- **Intisar Khanani** — *Thorn* (fairy tale retelling, slow-burn, character growth focus)\n' +
        "- **C.L. Polk** — *Witchmark* (mystery within magical society, angsty will-they-won't-they)\n" +
        '- **Emily Tesh** — *Silver in the Wood* (quiet, atmospheric, folk-horror romance)\n' +
        '- **Zen Cho** — *Sorcerer to the Crown* (Regency + magic + social commentary)\n' +
        '- **Erin Morgenstern** — *The Night Circus* (poetic, atmospheric, dual timeline, magical competition)\n' +
        '\n' +
        '## Input Types the System Must Handle\n' +
        '\n' +
        'These are the **runtime inputs** a user provides when invoking the fantasy-writer system. The user provides these, and the agent does everything else autonomously.\n' +
        '\n' +
        `1. **Story idea / concept seed** (REQUIRED): Brief description of a premise, "what if" scenario, or elevator pitch. Can range from a one-liner ("fae assassin falls for the prince she's sent to kill") to a multi-paragraph concept note.\n` +
        '2. **Reference stories / style samples** (OPTIONAL): Existing fiction passages to analyze for prose style, tone, pacing, voice. These are analyzed for abstract stylistic patterns ONLY — never reproduced or closely imitated in content. The system extracts structural characteristics (sentence rhythm, vocabulary register, metaphor density, POV style, paragraph cadence) and applies those patterns to wholly original prose.\n' +
        '3. **Mood / tone direction** (OPTIONAL): Tonal keywords or descriptions — dark gothic, whimsical cozy, politically tense, sensual slow-burn, grimdark, lyrical, etc.\n' +
        '4. **Character sketches** (OPTIONAL): Partial character ideas the user wants developed — names, traits, backstory fragments, relationship dynamics\n' +
        '5. **World fragments** (OPTIONAL): Partial worldbuilding — a magic system idea, a political structure, a geography, naming conventions\n' +
        '6. **Constraints** (OPTIONAL): Length targets (chapter count, word count), heat-level (fade-to-black through explicit), trope preferences (enemies-to-lovers, forced proximity, etc.), content warnings to include/avoid, standalone vs. series\n' +
        '\n' +
        'The minimum viable input is ONLY a story idea. Everything else is optional enrichment. The system must be able to generate a complete story from just a premise.\n' +
        '\n' +
        '## System Architecture Requirements\n' +
        '\n' +
        '### User-Facing Guide Agent\n' +
        'The system MUST include a **guide agent** as the sole user-facing entry point — identical in pattern to the fractal-factory-guide. The guide:\n' +
        '1. Asks the user for their story idea, optional style samples, mood/tone, character sketches, world fragments, and constraints\n' +
        '<exited with exit code 0>'
    },
