      'gen_ai.tool.call.arguments': '{"path":"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/domain-brief.md","view_range":[100,220]}',
      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '100. - **Culture & Daily Life**: Customs, food, clothing, languages (naming conventions), festivals, taboos\n' +
        '101. - **Fauna & Flora**: Magical creatures, dangerous flora, domesticated beasts\n' +
        '102. - **Consistency Bible**: A canonical reference document ensuring all worldbuilding details remain consistent\n' +
        '103. \n' +
        '104. ### Phase 3: Character Development\n' +
        '105. - **Protagonist Profiles**: Full character sheets for romantic leads — backstory, psychological wounds, desires (external goal + internal need), flaws, strengths, voice patterns, speech quirks\n' +
        '106. - **Romance Arc Design**: The specific dynamic between leads — what draws them together, what keeps them apart, what forces vulnerability, what is the emotional climax\n' +
        '107. - **Supporting Cast**: Named secondary characters with roles (mentor, rival, comic relief, betrayer), their own mini-arcs\n' +
        '108. - **Antagonist Design**: The villain or opposing force — motivation, methodology, relationship to protagonists, how they mirror/contrast the leads\n' +
        '109. - **Character Voice Guide**: Distinct speech patterns, internal monologue style, vocabulary level, emotional register for each POV character\n' +
        '110. - **Relationship Web**: How every named character relates to every other — alliances, tensions, secrets\n' +
        '111. \n' +
        '112. ### Phase 4: Plotting & Outlining\n' +
        '113. - **Story Structure Selection**: Choose and apply a structural framework:\n' +
        '114.   - **Three-Act Structure**: Setup → Confrontation → Resolution with clear act breaks\n' +
        '115.   - **Save the Cat Beat Sheet** (Blake Snyder): 15 beats from Opening Image through Final Image — particularly useful for pacing romance beats\n' +
        "116.   - **The Hero's Journey** (Campbell/Vogler): Call to adventure → Ordeal → Return — good for quest-heavy fantasy\n" +
        '117.   - **Seven-Point Story Structure** (Dan Wells): Hook → Plot Turn 1 → Pinch 1 → Midpoint → Pinch 2 → Plot Turn 2 → Resolution\n' +
        '118.   - **Romance Arc Structure**: Meet → Attraction → Rising conflict → Black moment → Grand gesture → Resolution (mapped alongside the fantasy plot arc)\n' +
        '119.   - **Dual-Arc Interleave**: The fantasy plot arc and romance arc must be plotted in parallel, with key beats from each reinforcing or complicating the other\n' +
        '120. - **Chapter-Level Outline**: Scene-by-scene breakdown with:\n' +
        '121.   - POV character\n' +
        '122.   - Scene goal (what the character wants)\n' +
        '123.   - Scene conflict (what opposes them)\n' +
        '124.   - Scene disaster/resolution (outcome that complicates)\n' +
        "125.   - Emotional arc (how the character's emotional state shifts)\n" +
        '126.   - Romance beat (where this scene falls on the romance arc)\n' +
        '127.   - Fantasy plot beat (where this falls on the main plot arc)\n' +
        '128.   - Key worldbuilding reveals\n' +
        '129. - **Tension Mapping**: Visual/textual pacing chart showing tension rise-and-fall across chapters\n' +
        '130. - **Subplot Tracking**: B-plots, C-plots with their own mini-arcs and intersection points with the main plot\n' +
        '131. \n' +
        '132. ### Phase 5: Prose Style Development\n' +
        '133. - **Voice Calibration**: If reference stories are provided, analyze them for:\n' +
        '134.   - Sentence length distribution (short punchy vs. long flowing)\n' +
        '135.   - Vocabulary level and register\n' +
        '136.   - Metaphor density and type (natural imagery, architectural, bodily, etc.)\n' +
        '137.   - Dialogue-to-narration ratio\n' +
        '138.   - Interior monologue style (close third, distant third, first person)\n' +
        '139.   - Paragraph rhythm and white space usage\n' +
        '140. - **Style Guide**: A concrete document specifying the target prose style for this particular story\n' +
        '141. - **Tone Palette**: Specific emotional tones by chapter type (action scenes = staccato/visceral; romantic scenes = sensory/languid; political scenes = precise/tense)\n' +
        '142. \n' +
        '143. ### Phase 6: Drafting\n' +
        '144. - **Chapter Drafting**: Write each chapter following the outline, maintaining:\n' +
        '145.   - Consistent character voice per POV\n' +
        '146.   - Scene structure (goal/conflict/disaster or goal/conflict/resolution)\n' +
        '147.   - Prose style adherence\n' +
        '148.   - Worldbuilding consistency\n' +
        '149.   - Romance arc pacing\n' +
        '150.   - Chapter hooks (opening hooks and closing cliffhangers/page-turners)\n' +
        "151. - **Continuity Tracking**: Track what each character knows at each point, where they physically are, timeline progression, promises made to the reader (Chekhov's guns)\n" +
        '152. - **Draft Segmentation**: Produce chapters in sequence, each building on the previous, with a running continuity document updated after each chapter\n' +
        '153. \n' +
        '154. ### Phase 7: Self-Editing & Revision (Multi-Pass, Exhaustive)\n' +
        '155. - **Developmental Edit Pass**: Review for:\n' +
        '156.   - Plot holes and logical inconsistencies\n' +
        '157.   - Pacing problems (sagging middle, rushed climax)\n' +
        '158.   - Character motivation gaps — does every action follow from established motivation?\n' +
        '159.   - Romance arc satisfaction — are beats earned? Is the progression natural?\n' +
        '160.   - Worldbuilding contradictions — does the magic work the same way it did 5 chapters ago?\n' +
        '161.   - Theme reinforcement — is the thematic argument landing?\n' +
        '162.   - Structural problems — scenes in wrong order, missing transitions, redundant scenes\n' +
        '163. - **Line Edit Pass**: Review for:\n' +
        '164.   - Prose quality and style consistency — flag every bland/generic sentence\n' +
        "165.   - Dialogue naturalism and character-voice distinctness — can you tell who's speaking without tags?\n" +
        '166.   - Overwriting (purple prose, info-dumping, unnecessary adjectives)\n' +
        '167.   - Underwriting (scenes that need more sensory/emotional detail)\n' +
        '168.   - Repetitive word/phrase usage — catch repeated sentence openings, pet phrases, overused words\n' +
        '169.   - Show vs. tell balance — flag every instance of told emotion in key scenes\n' +
        `170.   - Cliché detection — flag generic fantasy clichés ("orbs" for eyes, "smirk," "let out a breath they didn't know they were holding")\n` +
        "171.   - POV consistency — flag head-hopping, knowledge the POV character shouldn't have, wrong emotional register for the character\n" +
        '172. - **Copy Edit Pass**: \n' +
        '173.   - Grammar, punctuation, spelling\n' +
        '174.   - Consistency in naming, titles, place names, honorifics\n' +
        '175.   - Timeline consistency — day/night, travel distances, elapsed time\n' +
        "176.   - Factual accuracy within the world's rules\n" +
        '177. \n' +
        '178. ### Phase 8: Beta Reading Simulation (Deep, Multi-Perspective)\n' +
        '179. \n' +
        '180. This is a critical quality gate. The beta reading must be thorough enough to catch EVERY issue that would undermine reader satisfaction. It operates as multiple independent reviewers:\n' +
        '181. \n' +
        '182. - **Romance Reader Lens**:\n' +
        '183.   - Is the central relationship compelling? Are both leads equally developed?\n' +
        '184.   - Are the romantic beats (first meeting, first touch, first vulnerability, first conflict, reconciliation) satisfying and earned?\n' +
        '185.   - Is the chemistry believable — does the dialogue crackle, do the scenes between leads feel charged?\n' +
        '186.   - Is the heat level consistent with the stated target?\n' +
        '187.   - Does the HEA/HFN feel genuine, not forced?\n' +
        '188. \n' +
        '189. - **Fantasy Reader Lens**:\n' +
        '190.   - Is the worldbuilding immersive and internally consistent?\n' +
        '191.   - Is the magic system interesting and does it follow its own rules?\n' +
        '192.   - Are the stakes real? Does the fantasy plot have genuine tension?\n' +
        '193.   - Is the political/social structure believable?\n' +
        '194.   - Do the fantasy and romance arcs enhance each other, or does one feel bolted on?\n' +
        '195. \n' +
        '196. - **Craft Reader Lens** (most critical):\n' +
        '197.   - **Prose quality audit**: Flag every bland, generic, or lazy sentence. No "she felt a shiver run down her spine" defaults. Every metaphor must be fresh or at minimum genre-appropriate.\n' +
        '198.   - **Voice consistency audit**: For each POV character, verify the internal monologue voice is distinct and consistent across ALL their chapters. Flag drift.\n' +
        "199.   - **POV violation scan**: Flag every instance of head-hopping, knowledge leaks (character knows something they shouldn't), or tonal mismatches for the POV character.\n" +
        '200.   - **Continuity audit**: Cross-reference every fact (character location, time of day, weather, what characters know, physical descriptions, names mentioned) against the continuity tracker. Flag every inconsistency.\n' +
        "201.   - **Emotional authenticity audit**: For every major emotional beat, verify buildup, check that the reaction matches the character's established personality, and flag any reaction that feels generic rather than character-specific.\n" +
        "202.   - **Dialogue audit**: Verify each character's dialogue voice is distinct. Flag any passage where stripping speaker tags makes it impossible to identify who's talking.\n" +
        '203.   - **Pacing audit**: Map tension level per scene across the full manuscript. Flag pacing failures (three consecutive slow scenes, climax without adequate builds, anti-climactic resolutions).\n' +
        '204. \n' +
        '205. - **Sensitivity Reader Lens**:\n' +
        '206.   - Flag problematic representations, stereotypes, or harmful tropes\n' +
        '207.   - Check power dynamics in the romance for consent and agency\n' +
        '208.   - Review cultural elements for appropriation concerns\n' +
        '209. \n' +
        '210. - **Originality Reader Lens**:\n' +
        '211.   - Flag any passage, character, plot element, or world detail that too closely resembles a known published work\n' +
        '212.   - Cite the specific similarity and source\n' +
        '213.   - Verify the style guide was applied transformatively, not reproductively\n' +
        '214. \n' +
        '215. Each lens produces a structured findings document with severity levels (critical / major / minor / suggestion). All critical and major findings MUST be addressed in a revision pass before the chapter is considered complete.\n' +
        '216. \n' +
        '217. ### Phase 9: Polish & Delivery\n' +
        '218. - **Final proofread**: Last pass for typos, formatting, consistency\n' +
        '219. - **Chapter summaries**: One-paragraph summary per chapter for easy reference\n' +
        "220. - **Series knowledge base update** (if series): Promote this book's canonical facts into the series knowledge base — see Series Architecture below"
    },
