      'gen_ai.tool.call.result': '340. 3. **Previous book summaries** (`book-N/book-summary.md`) — for narrative context without re-reading full manuscripts\n' +
        '341. 4. **Unresolved threads** (`series/unresolved-threads.md`) — the sequel concept phase MUST decide which threads this book resolves, advances, or deliberately leaves open. No thread may be silently forgotten.\n' +
        '342. \n' +
        "343. The sequel does NOT need to re-read prior books' raw chapters — the series knowledge base and summaries are sufficient.\n" +
        '344. \n' +
        '345. ### Series Knowledge Base Lifecycle\n' +
        '346. \n' +
        '347. 1. **Book 1 (standalone)**: Per-book artifacts are created as usual. On completion, the book-summary agent extracts canonical facts into the `series/` directory. Even a "standalone" gets this treatment — it\'s cheap insurance.\n' +
        "348. 2. **Book N (sequel)**: The concept phase loads the series knowledge base. All phases cross-reference against it. On completion, the book-summary agent **merges** this book's new facts into the series knowledge base:\n" +
        '349.    - New characters → added to `character-registry.md`\n' +
        '350.    - Changed character states (injuries, alliances, deaths) → updated in registry\n' +
        '351.    - New world facts → merged into `world-bible.md`\n' +
        '352.    - Magic system expansion → merged into `magic-system.md` (must not contradict existing rules)\n' +
        '353.    - Resolved threads → moved from `unresolved-threads.md` to the book summary\n' +
        '354.    - New threads planted → added to `unresolved-threads.md` with the book they were planted in\n' +
        '355.    - Timeline events → appended to `timeline.md`\n' +
        '356. \n' +
        '357. ### Cross-Book Consistency Rules\n' +
        '358. \n' +
        '359. - The series knowledge base is **append-mostly**. Facts established in book 1 cannot be silently contradicted in book 2. If a retcon is needed, it must be narratively justified (unreliable narrator revealed, new information discovered by characters) and explicitly documented.\n' +
        "360. - Character voice guides carry across books. A character's voice in book 3 should be recognizably the same person as book 1, with natural evolution (trauma, growth, age) documented.\n" +
        '361. - The magic system can be *expanded* but not *contradicted*. New abilities must be compatible with established rules.\n' +
        '362. - The naming convention guide prevents naming collisions (two characters with similar names across books) and ensures consistent use of titles, honorifics, and place names.\n' +
        '363. \n' +
        '364. ## Craft Toolbox Selection\n' +
        '365. \n' +
        '366. During the **Concept** and **Plotting** phases, the system must produce a **Story Craft Profile** (`story-craft-profile.md`) that lists:\n' +
        '367. - Which tools from the Craft Toolbox (see invariants.md Part 2) are selected for this story\n' +
        '368. - Why each tool was selected (one sentence)\n' +
        '369. - Any tools explicitly NOT selected and why (brief)\n' +
        '370. - Any per-tool adjustments for this story (e.g., "T1 Scene-Sequel: applied to action/plot scenes; relaxed for romantic interludes")\n' +
        '371. \n' +
        '372. The adversarial auditors for all subsequent phases verify against the **selected** toolset, not the full toolbox. This ensures craft standards are applied with artistic judgment rather than mechanical rigidity.\n' +
        '373. \n' +
        '374. ## Output Artifacts\n' +
        '375. \n' +
        '376. The system produces per-book and series-level artifacts, each decomposed into subcategory files.\n' +
        '377. \n' +
        '378. ### Per-Book Artifact Directories\n' +
        '379. 1. **`concept/`** — premise, craft profile, constraints\n' +
        "380. 2. **`world/`** — this book's worldbuilding additions (geography, magic, politics, culture, history, flora/fauna — mirroring series structure)\n" +
        "381. 3. **`characters/`** — this book's character work + per-character arc plans and outcomes\n" +
        '382. 4. **`outline/`** — structural framework, per-chapter outlines, tension map, subplot tracker\n' +
        '383. 5. **`style/`** — prose style guide, tone palette, reference analysis\n' +
        '384. 6. **`chapters/drafts/`** — draft chapters, one file per chapter\n' +
        '385. 7. **`chapters/final/`** — polished final chapters\n' +
        '386. 8. **`continuity/`** — running tracker, character knowledge matrix, reader promises\n' +
        '387. 9. **`revision/`** — per-chapter findings by pass type (developmental, line-edit, copy-edit) + revision log\n' +
        '388. 10. **`beta-feedback/`** — per-lens findings (romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality) + synthesis\n' +
        '389. 11. **`killed-darlings.md`** — removed-but-saved passages, world details, plot elements\n' +
        '390. 12. **`book-summary.md`** — post-completion: plot summary, arc outcomes, state changes for series knowledge base\n' +
        '391. \n' +
        '392. ### Series-Level Artifact Directories (accumulated across books)\n' +
        '393. 13. **`series/world/`** — canonical worldbuilding (geography, magic, politics, culture, history, flora/fauna subdirectories)\n' +
        '394. 14. **`series/characters/`** — all characters (leads, supporting, antagonists, voice guides, registry index, relationship map)\n' +
        '395. 15. **`series/magic-system.md`** — high-level magic summary + links to `world/magic/` details\n' +
        '396. 16. **`series/timeline.md`** — cross-book chronological events\n' +
        "397. 17. **`series/unresolved-threads.md`** — planted Chekhov's guns not yet fired, tagged by origin book\n" +
        '398. 18. **`series/naming-conventions.md`** — established names, places, terms, honorifics\n' +
        '399. 19. **`series/style-guide.md`** — prose style continuity across books\n' +
        '400. \n' +
        '401. ## Quality Expectations\n' +
        '402. \n' +
        '403. - **Prose quality**: Publication-ready romantic fantasy prose — lush but not purple, emotionally resonant, genre-appropriate\n' +
        '404. - **Character depth**: Leads must feel like real people with complex inner lives, not archetypes\n' +
        '405. - **Romance satisfaction**: The relationship arc must feel earned — genuine tension, genuine vulnerability, genuine payoff\n' +
        '406. - **Worldbuilding coherence**: Every magical, political, and cultural detail must be internally consistent\n' +
        '407. - **Pacing**: Chapters should flow naturally with varied tension, avoiding both the "sagging middle" and rushed endings\n' +
        '408. - **Voice distinctness**: Each POV character must have a recognizably different internal voice\n' +
        '409. \n' +
        '410. ## Non-Goals\n' +
        '411. \n' +
        '412. - The system does NOT need to handle cover design, ISBN assignment, or publishing logistics\n' +
        '413. - The system does NOT need to market or promote the fiction\n' +
        '414. - The system does NOT generate illustrations or visual art (textual descriptions only)\n' +
        '415. - The system does NOT need to handle multiple simultaneous stories — one story at a time'
    },
